==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=Palooka}}
===Etymology===
Possibly from {{etyl|pl}} surname ''Paluka,'' or variant of ''[[Polack]].''<ref>“[http://www.worldwidewords.org/qa/qa-pal1.htm Palooka]”, [http://www.worldwidewords.org/ World Wide Words], Michael Quinion</ref>

US 1920s, originally primarily used of boxers. Popularized by Jack Conway of ''[[w:Variety (magazine)|Variety]],''<ref>''Esquire,'' September 1936</ref><ref>{{w|H. L. Mencken}}, 1945 supplement to ''{{w|The American Language}},'' reviewed in [http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,792388,00.html Books: Alphabet Soup], ''TIME,'' Monday, Aug. 27, 1945</ref> who also popularized {{term|baloney|lang=en}} and {{term|bimbo|lang=en}}. Further popularized by {{w|Ham Fisher}} in his comic strip ''{{w|Joe Palooka}},'' about a boxer (in newspapers from 1930, particularly popular in 1940s).<ref>''Cassell’s Dictionary Of Slang.'' Jonathon Green. Cassel & Co., 1998. ISBN: 0-304-35167-9</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{US}} {{slang}} a [[stupid]], [[oafish]] or [[clumsy]] person
# {{US}} an [[incompetent]] [[untalented]] [[boxer]], [[bridge]] player, etc.
#*'''1923''', ''{{w|Lincoln Star}},'' Nebraska, March 1923: 
#*:But [[w:Jack Dempsey|[Jack] Dempsey]] against some '''palooka''' who had been press agented into greatness and into the form of a Dempsey menace — that would pack any outdoor arena.

